  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Friday)\nIssued at 1059 AM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nUpper trough will remain in place through early next week over \nthe western CONUS, supporting mainly WSW flow aloft. Pattern \nchange expected toward next weekend as a strong NE Pac upper \ntrough dives into the Pac NW and supports a couple stronger upper \ntroughs and associated sfc low's across southern Canada and the \nnorthern CONUS, flattening the flow aloft. Sfc trough has pushed \nthrough most of the FA. A period of breezy winds can be expected \ninto the afternoon, esp in the lee of the Black Hills. Not much if\nany cool air with this front, just a drier airmass. Upper trough \nwill drop into the SW CONUS Sunday and direct tropical moisture NE\nacross the Rockies. Meanwhile low level SE return flow will \ndevelop, pushing LL moisture back west into the region. This will \nsupport much cooler conds on the SD Plains for Sunday, esp along \nthe east slopes of the BH where moist upslope flow will be in \nplace. Expect mostly cloudy skies there with light showers or \ndrizzle possible. Isolated light rain showers will also be \npossible across the remainder of the SD Plains as pos theta-e adv \nwill be ongoing through the day. Main lobe of deep moisture will \narrive in the evening via a lead impulse, supporting a good chance\nof showers and isolated TS. Another window of higher chances of \nshowers and storms can be expected Monday afternoon as the sfc \ntrough moves through the area, with a few stronger storms possible\non the SD Plains. Things dry out after Monday with seasonal temps\nexpected into the upper 60s to low 70s the rest of the week, lows\nin the 40s/50s. Models are still indicating a stronger cold front\nnext weekend, which could bring some frost as the upper trough \nover the NE Pac shifts east and forces cold air south out of \nCanada. However, the degree of CAA with this system still varies \nsubstantially among the various forecast models and ensembles, \nwith temperature spreads really large.",
